    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Background: Staphylococcus aureus is a leading pathogen and cause of orthopedic implant infections. S. aureus infections are often resistant to numerous antibiotic treatments and frequently grow in biofilm structures. Biofilms occur when microorganisms adhere to surfaces and grow in networks that are phenotypically different from planktonic counterparts. These differences make biofilms resistant to traditional antibiotic treatment. Biofilms actively modulate their redox environments suggesting the utility of a potent small molecule redox-active compound in treating biofilm infections. \r\n\r\nMethods: S. aureus (ATCC 29213) biofilms were established and were then treated with either a potent redox-active metalloporphyrin MnTE-2-PyP (chemical name: manganese (II) meso-tetrakis-(N-methylpyridinium-2-yl) porphyrin) or phosphate-buffered saline (PBS). The matrix was quantified with crystal violet and confocal microscopy. An animal model was conducted using an S. aureus infected intramedually fixed femur fracture. This murine model ran over a two week time course and mice were treated with either no treatment, MnTE-2-PyP, antibiotic, or a combined therapy of antibiotic and MnTE-2- PyP. Femurs were collected at the experimental endpoint and were analyzed for CFUs. \r\n\r\nResults: MnTE-2-PyP was found to significantly reduce the extracellular matrix in the in vitro studies. In the murine fracture model, combined MnTE-2-PyP -antibiotic treatment resulted in lower CFU/g bacteria counts in harvested femurs as compared with the other treatment groups.\r\n\r\nConclusions: Changes in redox environment seem to have significant effects on biofilm structure and robustness. Utilization of a redox-active metalloporphyrin in conjunction with antibiotics may be an effective therapeutic possibility in treating biofilm infections.",
